Dear Principal, Many kids all over the country talk on cell phones. We use them to communicate with one another. We also use cell phones for emergencies. For example if are out with your friends and you need to talk with one of your parents what do u do? You call them or text them. All though you can use them for urgent cares, students at school and adults at work can get distracted bye a cell phone. Policy one states, students are allowed to bring cell phones to school and only use them during lunch and passing period but phones have to be turned off during class time. Policy two states, students are not allowed to have cell phones at all. Policy two is a better choice because have a cell phone can be extremely distracting to students and maybe even teachers. School is a department of education to learn new things and life long skills. Students also might get hurt texting on a phone they will be to busy to pay attention to a flight of stairs two steps away. If you decide policy one you might have complaints about cell phones ringing during the middle of class which does not just alarm the owner but also the rest of the class. By picking policy two you have eliminated the disruption for students and have made it easer for students to learn as much as possible.
Dear principal, I think that you should follow up with policy number one which states that students are allowed to bring cell phones to school and use them during lunch periods and free times, as long as the phones are turned off during class time. I use the core democratic value of Pursuit of Happiness to fit my position. The core democratic value of the Pursuit of Happiness states that a person can do whatever it is that makes them happy without harming anyone else or something else. In this case this students can use their cell phones as long as their class time isn't affected by the cell phones. What I'm trying to say is that if you ban cell phones from being in the school at all times then the students will try to bring cell phones and play with it during class time. In that case, that will affect the students learning habits and education. You should be reminded that you have the power to make rules concerning cell phone usage! If you still feel strongly about the other policy you can make letters to all parents and children to tell you what they think about both polices. I have expressed my feelings to you greatly. Sincerely, STUDENT_NAME
